/* Dr. Lam, oral disorders (Lecture 13, taught 11/20), Exam 3: tooth hypersensitivity versus toothache, canker sores, cold sores, cold sore versus canker sore, dry mouth. Ids LOR-*.
   Sources: Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf (slide N = PDF page, 84 slides), her handout Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf, and DEENT_Lecture_11.20_transcript.txt (T@n = character offset, cited "11/20").
   Source notes: the protectant disc time is 10-20 seconds on slide 37 and 10-20 minutes in the handout and recording, so only LOR-070 (lowYield) asks it. Benzocaine is 10-20% on slide 34 and
   5-20% in the handout, and she gave no number, so no item keys a benzocaine strength. Oral hydrogen peroxide: 1.5% direct application and 3% mixed with equal water (slide and handout); she was unsure aloud, so no item asks the strength.
   Carbamide peroxide direct application is 1 minute on the slide and handout and 1 or 2 minutes aloud, so no item keys the minutes. The slide-79 dry mouth exclusion list is self-study (she did not read it) and is not asked.
   Slide 49 lists recurrence as an exclusion, but her second case (outbreak a year ago, already seen by a doctor) is a self-care candidate; items key only the exclusions that are not in doubt (no prior diagnosis, more than 14 days, systemic signs) and that case as she keyed it. */
QUESTIONS.push(
{id:"LOR-001", skill:"patho", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"path", concept:"lr-tooth-patho-dentin",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Reyes, 34, has a brief, sharp pain when she drinks ice water. It stops as soon as she swallows. Which change in the tooth best explains this pain?",
 options:[
  {t:"Reduced salivary flow has left the teeth without protective saliva from the mouth", correct:false, why:"Dry mouth raises the risk of dental caries and presents with difficulty talking and chewing. It does not explain pain on cold exposure."},
  {t:"Dentin tubules are open to the mouth and pulp after enamel loss", correct:true, why:"Loss of enamel or gum recession exposes dentin, so the tubules are open to the oral cavity and the pulp. A stimulus then reaches the nerve and the pain is brief."},
  {t:"Bacteria have invaded the pulp and set off an inflammatory response", correct:false, why:"This is toothache. Its pain remains even without a stimulus, which does not match pain that stops after the cold drink."},
  {t:"A latent herpesvirus has reactivated at the junction of the lip and skin", correct:false, why:"This describes a cold sore, a lesion outside the mouth. It does not cause a sharp pain triggered by a cold drink."}],
 teach:"Hypersensitivity begins when enamel is lost or the gum recedes, leaving open dentin tubules. Heat, cold, pressure or acid then reach the nerve and cause a quick, sharp pain that stops when the stimulus is gone. Toothache is a bacterial invasion with pain that continues without a stimulus.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 7, 9; T@46304, T@55813 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-002", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"path", concept:"lr-tooth-toothache-id",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Okafor, 52, has a dull, throbbing pain in a back tooth that continues through the day whether he eats, drinks or rests. Which condition and cause fit?",
 options:[
  {t:"Herpes simplex labialis, from reactivation of latent HSV-1", correct:false, why:"A cold sore appears on or around the lips as red fluid-filled vesicles. It is not a deep tooth pain."},
  {t:"Tooth hypersensitivity, from exposed and open dentin tubules", correct:false, why:"Hypersensitivity pain occurs on exposure to a stimulus and stops when it is removed. This pain continues at rest, so the stem does not fit."},
  {t:"Toothache, from bacterial invasion causing an inflammatory response", correct:true, why:"Pain that remains in the absence of a stimulus points to toothache. The cause is invading bacteria and the inflammatory response they trigger."},
  {t:"Recurrent aphthous stomatitis, from local trauma or a nutritional deficiency", correct:false, why:"A canker sore is an ulcer on the mouth lining with a prodrome. It is not a throbbing pain in a tooth."}],
 teach:"Ask whether the pain needs a trigger. Pain on exposure that is gone when the stimulus stops is hypersensitivity. Pain that stays without any stimulus, throbbing and constant, is toothache from bacterial invasion. A pharmacist cannot treat the bacteria, so the patient needs a dentist.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 9; T@46304, T@55813 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-003", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"case", concept:"lr-tooth-case1-referral",
 tags:["poll", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"A 56-year-old patient presents with throbbing tooth pain in the back of the mouth. The pain started two days ago and nothing makes it worse or better. Past medical history includes GERD (controlled with Nexium OTC). He brushes his teeth once a day in the evening with a regular toothbrush. Select the most appropriate intervention for this patient.",
 options:[
  {t:"Counsel the patient on minimizing GERD symptoms", correct:false, why:"Controlling reflux protects enamel, but this pain is not triggered by exposure. It does not address a toothache."},
  {t:"Recommend a soft-bristle toothbrush with standard fluoride toothpaste", correct:false, why:"This is the first step for hypersensitivity. It does not treat a bacterial toothache with constant pain."},
  {t:"Recommend a toothpaste that contains potassium nitrate 5%", correct:false, why:"A desensitizing paste is for pain on exposure and is added after the soft-brush step. It would not help a toothache."},
  {t:"Recommend a dental referral", correct:true, why:"Pain that remains with no stimulus is toothache from bacterial invasion. Nothing over the counter helps the symptoms, so the dentist must treat it."}],
 teach:"This is her own case: pain with no stimulus is toothache, which is an exclusion to self-care. Her point was that nothing over the counter helps, so counseling on GERD or brushing misses the problem. The right step is to send him to a dentist.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 11, 20, 21; T@53054, T@46667 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-004", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"case", concept:"lr-tooth-toothache-nostimulus",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Tran, 47, has had a dull, throbbing ache in a lower molar for 3 days, even at rest. She asks you for a toothpaste with potassium nitrate. What is the best response?",
 options:[
  {t:"Refer her to a dentist, since pain at rest points to toothache", correct:true, why:"Toothache is an exclusion to self-care. The bacteria must be cleaned out by a dentist, which a pharmacist cannot do."},
  {t:"Recommend an arginine 8% toothpaste to use twice daily", correct:false, why:"Arginine seals exposed dentin. Her ache is not triggered by exposure, so sealing dentin would not relieve it."},
  {t:"Advise fewer acidic drinks and recheck her in 4 to 6 weeks", correct:false, why:"Waiting 4 to 6 weeks applies to desensitizing toothpaste for sensitivity. A toothache needs a dentist now."},
  {t:"Start a soft-bristle brush with standard fluoride toothpaste for 7 days", correct:false, why:"This is the first step for pain on exposure to hot or cold. Her pain continues at rest, so it is not hypersensitivity."}],
 teach:"Her request for potassium nitrate fits a sensitivity problem, but the pain at rest says toothache. Toothache is on the exclusion list, and there is nothing a pharmacist can give to treat it. Refer, rather than start the desensitizing steps.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 9, 11; T@46304, T@46667 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-005", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"excl", concept:"lr-tooth-severe-cold",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"recording",
 stem:"Mr. Dube, 60, has receding gums from brushing too hard. He reports very severe pain whenever he drinks anything cold. What is the best next step?",
 options:[
  {t:"Advise waiting 30 to 60 minutes to brush after acidic food or drinks, and brush softly", correct:false, why:"This is a non-drug measure for mild sensitivity from acid. It does not address severe pain from recession."},
  {t:"Refer him to a dentist for possible deep cleaning of the recession", correct:true, why:"Severe pain triggered by cold is on the exclusion list. She said it can mean so much recession that a dentist must do a deep cleaning."},
  {t:"Start a soft-bristle brush with standard fluoride toothpaste for 7 days", correct:false, why:"This step is for mild pain on exposure. Her exam wording for severe pain is to send the patient to a dentist."},
  {t:"Recommend a potassium nitrate 5% toothpaste twice daily for 4 to 6 weeks of use", correct:false, why:"A desensitizing paste treats mild sensitivity. Severe pain with cold is outside what the pharmacist can treat."}],
 teach:"Severity decides the path. Mild pain with a stimulus can be treated with the stepwise plan. When the pain is severe, especially with cold drinks, she said recession may be advanced and a dentist must deep clean. She said she will write the word severe on the exam.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 11; T@55530, T@56064 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-006", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"case", concept:"lr-tooth-case2-select", multi:true,
 tags:["poll", "multi", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"A 26-year-old woman presents with short, sharp tooth pain when she drinks hot or cold beverages. The pain is mild and started about two days ago. She has been drinking lemonade daily with lunch and orange juice with breakfast. She brushes twice a day with a regular toothbrush and fluoride toothpaste. She saw her dentist one month ago with no concerns. SELECT ALL the appropriate initial interventions for this patient.",
 options:[
  {t:"Drink less acidic beverages", correct:true, why:"Acidic drinks are a predisposing factor for exposed dentin. Removing them is the first non-drug step."},
  {t:"Brush with potassium nitrate toothpaste", correct:false, why:"She said it is not time yet. The guidelines start with the soft brush and standard fluoride, then add the desensitizing paste after a week."},
  {t:"Use a soft-bristle toothbrush with standardized fluoride toothpaste", correct:true, why:"This is the initial regimen: soft brush with standard fluoride for 7 days, before a desensitizing paste."},
  {t:"Take acid-suppressing therapy to prevent GERD", correct:false, why:"Reflux is a factor in enamel erosion, but this patient has no GERD. Her described exposure is acidic drinks."},
  {t:"Recommend contacting her dentist", correct:false, why:"Mild pain on exposure, with a recent dental visit, is treatable. A referral is for toothache or severe pain."}],
 teach:"Her pain comes on with exposure and is mild, so this is hypersensitivity that the pharmacist treats. She wanted the guideline order: cut the acid, switch to a soft brush with standard fluoride for a week, and add a desensitizing paste if that fails.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 22, 23, 14, 15; T@53915, T@54320 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-007", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"case", concept:"lr-tooth-mild-stepone",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Silva, 29, has mild, short, sharp pain with iced coffee that began 2 days ago. He drinks two sodas a day and brushes right after breakfast with a firm-bristle brush. Which is the best first intervention?",
 options:[
  {t:"Refer him to a dentist because pain with cold signals a toothache, not sensitivity", correct:false, why:"His pain is mild and occurs with a stimulus, which is hypersensitivity. Toothache would not depend on a stimulus."},
  {t:"Start a potassium nitrate 5% toothpaste and keep his firm-bristle brush", correct:false, why:"A desensitizing paste is the second step. She said it is added after the first week of the soft brush and standard fluoride."},
  {t:"Recommend an arginine 8% toothpaste and keep brushing after meals", correct:false, why:"Arginine is also a second-step ingredient, and brushing right after acidic food and drinks should wait 30 to 60 minutes."},
  {t:"Use a soft-bristle brush with standard fluoride for 7 days and cut acidic drinks", correct:true, why:"The guideline starts with the soft brush and standard fluoride for 7 days, and removes the acid exposure. His brush and drinks are the predisposing factors."}],
 teach:"Mild pain on exposure is treated in steps. Step one is a soft or low-abrasion brush with standard fluoride for 7 days and removal of acid exposure. Desensitizing paste is step two. She taught this guideline order even though people often skip to the paste.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 14, 15, 22, 23; T@48832, T@54320 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-008", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"pharm", concept:"lr-tooth-steptwo",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Park, 40, finished 7 days of a soft-bristle brush with standard fluoride toothpaste. Her pain with cold drinks is the same. She has no exclusion criteria. What is the next management step?",
 options:[
  {t:"Soft-bristle brush with a desensitizing toothpaste", correct:true, why:"After 7 days of the first step without improvement, the next management is the soft brush plus a desensitizing toothpaste."},
  {t:"Refer her to a dentist right away", correct:false, why:"She has no exclusion criteria, so she is still a candidate for the second step. A dentist is for exclusions or persistent pain after treatment."},
  {t:"Start acid-suppressing therapy to prevent GERD", correct:false, why:"She has no GERD in the stem. Acid control applies to a patient whose reflux is eroding enamel."},
  {t:"Repeat the soft-bristle brush and standard fluoride for another 7 days", correct:false, why:"The first step was completed without benefit, so repeating it is not the plan. The next management adds a desensitizing paste."}],
 teach:"The plan has two steps, each tested before the next. Step one is a soft brush with standard fluoride for 7 days. If that has been done and the pain persists, step two is a soft brush with a desensitizing toothpaste. A dentist visit follows if pain persists or worsens.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 15; T@48832, T@49038 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-009", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"excl", concept:"lr-tooth-excl-single",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which patient has an exclusion to self-care for sensitive teeth?",
 options:[
  {t:"A woman with short, sharp pain on cold drinks who brushes twice daily with a soft brush", correct:false, why:"This is mild hypersensitivity with a stimulus. It is a candidate for the stepwise plan, not an exclusion."},
  {t:"A man with fever and facial swelling near a sore tooth", correct:true, why:"Fever or swelling is on the exclusion list. It suggests infection, which a dentist must treat."},
  {t:"An adult with controlled GERD and fleeting pain on cold air", correct:false, why:"Controlled reflux and fleeting pain on exposure fit hypersensitivity. Reflux control is a non-drug measure."},
  {t:"A teen with mild pain on hot drinks who has orange juice daily", correct:false, why:"Mild pain with a stimulus and an acidic habit is treatable. Cutting acid is part of the plan."}],
 teach:"Her exam form is: does this patient have an exclusion, yes or no, and which one. Exclusions include toothache, fever or swelling, trauma with bleeding, loose or broken teeth, bleeding gums, severe pain with hot, cold or chewing, and sore mouth from poorly fitting dentures.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 11; T@46859, T@46667 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-010", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"excl", concept:"lr-tooth-excl-select", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi"], source:"both",
 stem:"SELECT ALL findings that mean a patient with a sensitive tooth should see a dentist instead of self-treating.",
 options:[
  {t:"Mouth soreness from poorly fitting dentures", correct:true, why:"Mouth soreness associated with poorly fitting dentures is on the exclusion list."},
  {t:"Short, sharp pain with an iced drink that stops right away", correct:false, why:"This is hypersensitivity, which is treated with the soft brush and standard fluoride first."},
  {t:"Brushing twice daily with a regular toothbrush", correct:false, why:"This is a habit to change to a soft brush. It is not an exclusion."},
  {t:"Bleeding gums", correct:true, why:"Bleeding gums are an exclusion to self-care, so the patient is referred."},
  {t:"Loose teeth", correct:true, why:"Loose teeth are on the exclusion list. She said there is nothing a pharmacist can give to treat this."}],
 teach:"Exclusions are findings of disease the pharmacist cannot treat: toothache, fever or swelling, loose, broken or knocked-out teeth, bleeding gums, trauma, severe pain with hot, cold or chewing, and denture soreness. Mild pain on exposure and a regular brush are the things the plan fixes.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 11; T@46859, T@46667 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-011", skill:"class", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"pharm", concept:"lr-tooth-actives-recognize",
 tags:["hard", "recognize"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which list names four active ingredients found in desensitizing toothpastes?",
 options:[
  {t:"Sodium fluoride, benzocaine, stannous fluoride and arginine", correct:false, why:"Benzocaine is a topical anesthetic for canker sores and cold sores, not a desensitizing active."},
  {t:"Potassium nitrate, carbamide peroxide, sodium fluoride and arginine", correct:false, why:"Carbamide peroxide is an oral debriding agent for canker sores. It does not treat dentin sensitivity."},
  {t:"Potassium nitrate, docosanol, stannous fluoride and arginine", correct:false, why:"Docosanol is the first-line cold sore antiviral. It is not a toothpaste ingredient."},
  {t:"Potassium nitrate, sodium fluoride, stannous fluoride and arginine", correct:true, why:"These are the actives on her slide. She said to recognize them and gave no brand names."}],
 teach:"She said all you need to know is that these are the common desensitizing actives: potassium nitrate 5%, sodium fluoride, stannous fluoride and arginine 8%. She gives no brand names, because brands carry different ingredients, so read the active on the label.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 15; T@49358, T@49448 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-012", skill:"adverse", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"pharm", concept:"lr-tooth-kno3-child",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"A mother asks for a desensitizing toothpaste for her 10-year-old son. Which active ingredient should you tell her to avoid?",
 options:[
  {t:"Potassium nitrate 5%", correct:true, why:"Potassium nitrate is the ingredient not for children under 12 years old. Sodium fluoride, stannous fluoride and arginine carry no age limit on her slide."},
  {t:"Stannous fluoride toothpaste", correct:false, why:"Stannous fluoride is a desensitizing active without the under-12 restriction on the slide."},
  {t:"Sodium fluoride toothpaste", correct:false, why:"Sodium fluoride is a desensitizing active without an age limit on her slide."},
  {t:"Arginine 8% toothpaste", correct:false, why:"Arginine seals exposed dentin and has no age limit on her slide."}],
 teach:"Potassium nitrate 5% is the active she singled out: it is not for children under 12. For a 10-year-old, read the label and choose a product without potassium nitrate, after the first step of a soft brush with standard fluoride.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 15; T@49740 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-013", skill:"mech", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"pharm", concept:"lr-tooth-kno3-moa",
 tags:["hard"], source:"slide",
 stem:"What is the mechanism of potassium nitrate 5% in a desensitizing toothpaste?",
 options:[
  {t:"It seals the exposed dentin to minimize the flow of fluid", correct:false, why:"This is the mechanism of arginine 8%, listed in the second row of the slide table."},
  {t:"It depolarizes the excited nerves in the tubules", correct:true, why:"Potassium nitrate acts on the excited nerves in the dentin tubules, so the nerves stop firing in response to stimuli."},
  {t:"It inhibits fusion of the virus with the cell membrane", correct:false, why:"This is docosanol, the cold sore antiviral."},
  {t:"It replaces protective components that saliva lacks", correct:false, why:"This is the purpose of artificial saliva in dry mouth."}],
 teach:"Her slide groups desensitizing actives by mechanism. Potassium nitrate (with the fluorides) depolarizes the excited nerves in the tubules. Arginine 8% seals the exposed dentin to minimize fluid flow. The mechanisms of cold sore and dry mouth products belong to other conditions.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 15"},

{id:"LOR-014", skill:"mech", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"pharm", concept:"lr-tooth-arginine-moa",
 tags:["hard"], source:"slide",
 stem:"A patient asks which ingredient in a desensitizing paste seals exposed dentin so fluid flow in the tubules is minimized. Which is it?",
 options:[
  {t:"Carbamide peroxide 10-15%", correct:false, why:"This is an oral debriding agent for canker sores, not a toothpaste ingredient."},
  {t:"Docosanol 10%", correct:false, why:"This is a cold sore antiviral cream, not a desensitizing toothpaste ingredient."},
  {t:"Arginine 8%", correct:true, why:"The slide lists arginine as the active that minimizes fluid flow by sealing exposed dentin."},
  {t:"Potassium nitrate 5%", correct:false, why:"Potassium nitrate depolarizes excited nerves and is not for children under 12."}],
 teach:"Two mechanisms on the slide: depolarizing the excited nerves in the tubules, and minimizing fluid flow by sealing the exposed dentin. Arginine 8% is the sealing active. Recognizing the active and its job lets you read any brand.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 15"},

{id:"LOR-015", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"couns", concept:"lr-tooth-single-use",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Evans, 45, brushed once with a desensitizing toothpaste last night and says it did not help. What is the best counseling?",
 options:[
  {t:"Wait 4 to 6 weeks before using it again", correct:false, why:"The 4 to 6 weeks is how long to keep using it to see the benefit. It is not a gap between uses."},
  {t:"Use a half-inch length on the tongue and spread it around the mouth", correct:false, why:"This is the artificial saliva gel instruction for dry mouth, not toothpaste."},
  {t:"Stop it and return to a soft brush with standard fluoride for 7 days", correct:false, why:"That was the first step. Stopping after one night does not give the paste a fair trial."},
  {t:"Use it continuously, because a single application has no effect", correct:true, why:"She set this expectation: one-time use gives no benefit and the symptoms improve with continuous use."}],
 teach:"Set the expectation up front: a single application has no effect, and relief comes from continuous use. Brush with a 1-inch strip at least twice a day for about 4 to 6 weeks, and continue until the sensitivity subsides or as long as the dentist recommends.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 17; T@50423, T@50793 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-016", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"couns", concept:"lr-tooth-regimen",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which instruction for using a desensitizing toothpaste is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"Use a 1-inch strip at least twice daily, long term", correct:true, why:"This matches slide 17: continuous use, a 1-inch strip, at least twice daily, over about 4 to 6 weeks or as the dentist advises."},
  {t:"Use a 1-inch strip once daily for 7 days and then stop using it", correct:false, why:"Seven days is the length of the first step with standard fluoride. The desensitizing paste is used at least twice daily for weeks."},
  {t:"Use a 1-inch strip when the pain starts, and stop when it eases", correct:false, why:"Single uses have no effect. The paste works with continuous use, not just during pain."},
  {t:"Use a half-inch strip three to four times a day after each meal", correct:false, why:"Three to four times a day after meals is the schedule for protectants and anesthetics. The toothpaste is a 1-inch strip twice daily."}],
 teach:"Desensitizing toothpaste is a long-term, regular product: a 1-inch strip on a soft brush at least twice daily for about 4 to 6 weeks. Taking it as needed, or for a week like the first step, does not give the effect.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 17; T@50656, T@50793 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-017", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"couns", concept:"lr-tooth-followup",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Bailey, 38, has used a potassium nitrate toothpaste correctly, twice daily with a soft brush, for 7 weeks. Her sensitivity is not better. What should you recommend?",
 options:[
  {t:"Switch back to standard fluoride toothpaste for 7 days", correct:false, why:"That is the first step, which would come before a desensitizing paste, not after a failed course."},
  {t:"Refer her to a dentist for further evaluation", correct:true, why:"If the pain persists or worsens, or new symptoms develop after the 4 to 6 weeks of treatment, the follow-up is a dentist visit."},
  {t:"Add a protectant patch three to four times a day after meals", correct:false, why:"Protectant patches are for canker sores. They are not part of the plan for tooth sensitivity."},
  {t:"Continue the same toothpaste for another 4 to 6 weeks", correct:false, why:"She has already completed the treatment period correctly. Continuing without evaluation does not follow the follow-up plan."}],
 teach:"Follow-up is built into the plan: refer to a dentist if the pain persists or worsens, or if new symptoms develop after 4 to 6 weeks. She also said everyone should see a dentist at least every 6 months, because pharmacists can do little for dental disease.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 18; T@50793, T@51001, T@51108 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-018", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"couns", concept:"lr-tooth-brush-timing",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Ortiz, 31, drinks orange juice with breakfast and brushes right after. She has sensitive teeth. Which advice is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"Brush for 30 to 60 minutes with a potassium nitrate toothpaste", correct:false, why:"The 30 to 60 minutes is the wait before brushing, not how long to brush."},
  {t:"Swish with 3% hydrogen peroxide right after the juice", correct:false, why:"Hydrogen peroxide is a canker sore debriding agent and can sensitize teeth. It is not the advice for acid exposure."},
  {t:"Wait 30 to 60 minutes after acid before brushing", correct:true, why:"Acid softens the enamel and brushing right away adds abrasion. Brushing should wait 30 to 60 minutes after acidic food or drinks."},
  {t:"Brush right after with a stiffer brush to remove the acid", correct:false, why:"Abrasion is the problem. The slide advises a low-abrasion, soft brush and a wait after acid."}],
 teach:"Eliminate predisposing factors. Acid softens the enamel, so avoid brushing for 30 to 60 minutes after acidic food or drinks, and use a soft or low-abrasion brush with standard fluoride. Less acid and less abrasion are the point.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 14; T@47988, T@48128 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-019", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-tooth", sub:"couns", concept:"lr-tooth-gerd",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Hale, 58, has untreated GERD and sensitive teeth with enamel erosion. Which measure addresses a predisposing factor?",
 options:[
  {t:"Use an arginine 8% paste in place of standard fluoride toothpaste", correct:false, why:"Arginine is part of the second step. It does not control reflux or its erosion."},
  {t:"Brush within 15 minutes of each reflux episode with a firm brush", correct:false, why:"The wait after acid exposure is 30 to 60 minutes, and the brush should be soft, not firm."},
  {t:"Rinse with a baking soda paste after each meal", correct:false, why:"Baking soda paste is a canker sore option. It is not the tooth sensitivity measure."},
  {t:"Minimize his GERD symptoms to limit enamel erosion", correct:true, why:"Acid reflux erodes the enamel. She said treating the reflux is how to prevent further erosion."}],
 teach:"Eliminate predisposing factors: acidic food and drinks, hard brushing, and GERD. For the patient with reflux, treating the acid is what prevents more enamel loss, so counseling on reflux belongs in the non-drug plan alongside the soft brush.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 14; T@48713 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-020", skill:"patho", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"etio", concept:"lr-ras-cause-unknown",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Kim, 22, asks what causes her canker sores. Which statement is accurate?",
 options:[
  {t:"The cause is unknown; trauma, nutrition and drugs are triggers", correct:true, why:"She said the exact cause is not known, but there are triggers: local trauma, systemic or immunologic conditions, nutritional conditions, medications and others."},
  {t:"They are caused by bacterial invasion of the tooth pulp", correct:false, why:"This is toothache. Canker sores are ulcers on the mouth lining."},
  {t:"They result from loss of enamel that exposes the dentin tubules", correct:false, why:"This is tooth hypersensitivity, a different condition."},
  {t:"They are caused by HSV-1, which goes latent and then reactivates", correct:false, why:"This is the cause of a cold sore. Canker sores have no known infectious cause."}],
 teach:"Canker sores (recurrent aphthous stomatitis) have no known exact cause. Precipitating factors are local trauma, systemic or immunologic disease, nutritional deficiency, medications and others. The cold sore is the one caused by HSV-1.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 26; T@57025 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-021", skill:"patho", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"etio", concept:"lr-ras-triggers-select", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi"], source:"both",
 stem:"SELECT ALL precipitating factors for recurrent aphthous stomatitis (canker sores).",
 options:[
  {t:"Iron or B vitamin deficiency", correct:true, why:"Nutritional conditions such as deficiencies in iron, B vitamins or folic acid can precipitate canker sores."},
  {t:"Reactivation of latent HSV-1", correct:false, why:"This triggers a cold sore, a lesion outside the mouth."},
  {t:"Open dentin tubules after enamel loss", correct:false, why:"This is the pathophysiology of tooth hypersensitivity, not canker sores."},
  {t:"Biting the inside of the cheek", correct:true, why:"Local trauma, such as biting the cheek or irritation from braces, is a precipitating factor."},
  {t:"Chemotherapy", correct:true, why:"Medications are a listed precipitating factor, and the handout names chemotherapy among them."}],
 teach:"The handout groups the triggers: local trauma, systemic or immunologic conditions, nutritional conditions, medications, and others such as stress and food. Latent viral reactivation belongs to cold sores, and exposed dentin tubules belong to tooth hypersensitivity.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 26; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@57025 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-022", skill:"patho", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"pres", concept:"lr-ras-prodrome",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient says that the spot inside her lip tingles and burns the day before an ulcer shows up. What is this called, and how long before the lesion can it occur?",
 options:[
  {t:"A secondary infection, which occurs 2 to 48 hours after the lesion", correct:false, why:"Secondary infection is a cold sore finding when the lesion fails to crust. It is not a warning sensation."},
  {t:"A prodrome, which can occur 7 to 14 days before the lesion appears", correct:false, why:"Seven to 14 days is how long the lesion takes to heal on its own, not the warning period."},
  {t:"A prodrome, which occurs 2 to 48 hours before the lesion", correct:true, why:"She defined prodrome as the burning or prickling sensation that comes before the lesion, 2 to 48 hours ahead in the handout."},
  {t:"A trigger, which occurs 7 to 14 days after the lesion appears", correct:false, why:"A trigger precedes the sore. The healing time is 7 to 14 days."}],
 teach:"Some people feel a pricking or burning sensation, the prodrome, about 2 to 48 hours before the canker sore appears. The ulcer is then round or oval and heals by itself in 7 to 14 days. Pain can increase with eating and drinking.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 27; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@58747 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-023", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"assess", concept:"lr-ras-goal",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Lee, 25, has a first minor canker sore that began 3 days ago. What is the main goal of treatment?",
 options:[
  {t:"Repair the damaged tooth surface and minimize abrasion of the enamel", correct:false, why:"These are goals for tooth hypersensitivity."},
  {t:"Reduce the duration of the active infection and curtail its spread", correct:false, why:"These are cold sore goals, since HSV-1 infection is contagious."},
  {t:"Relieve dry mouth and reduce the risk of dental decay and cavities", correct:false, why:"These are goals for xerostomia."},
  {t:"Relieve pain and prevent complications while it heals", correct:true, why:"She said that because it is self-resolving, the goal is to provide pain relief. Preventing complications such as secondary infection is the other goal."}],
 teach:"Goals follow the condition. Canker sore: pain relief and prevention of complications, since the lesion resolves in 7 to 14 days on its own. Cold sore: shorten the infection, relieve pain, prevent secondary infection and limit spread. Each product answers one of those goals.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 30, 48; T@59337, T@74363 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-024", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"assess", concept:"lr-ras-refer-14",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"A 30-year-old has had a round gray ulcer on the inside of his cheek for 3 weeks. A rinse he tried did not help. What is the best action?",
 options:[
  {t:"Refer him to a provider, since the lesion has lasted over 14 days", correct:true, why:"A canker sore should heal in 7 to 14 days. A lesion present more than 14 days can mean another cause and is an exclusion to self-care."},
  {t:"Recommend a carbamide peroxide product for another 7 days of use", correct:false, why:"The debriding agents are limited to 7 days, and he has already passed 14 days with the lesion."},
  {t:"Recommend ice in 10-minute increments, up to 20 minutes each hour", correct:false, why:"Ice is a comfort measure for a new lesion. It does not answer an ulcer that has outlasted the normal healing time."},
  {t:"Recommend docosanol 10% five times a day for up to 10 days", correct:false, why:"Docosanol treats a cold sore. A gray ulcer inside the cheek is a canker sore."}],
 teach:"Her first decision is treat or refer. A canker sore normally lasts 7 to 14 days by itself, so a lesion present more than 14 days, as in this stem, needs a provider. She also listed frequent recurrence, systemic illness and failed self-care.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 29, 40; T@59576 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-025", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"assess", concept:"lr-ras-refer-select", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"SELECT ALL findings in a patient with canker sores that call for referral to a healthcare provider.",
 options:[
  {t:"A lesion that has been present for 16 days", correct:true, why:"Lesions present more than 14 days are an exclusion to self-care."},
  {t:"Frequently recurring lesions in a patient with celiac disease", correct:true, why:"She linked frequent recurrence to conditions that cause nutritional deficiency, such as celiac disease or short bowel syndrome."},
  {t:"Pain that increases with eating and drinking", correct:false, why:"Pain with eating is expected, and relief of pain is the goal of treatment."},
  {t:"A first ulcer that tingled before it appeared", correct:false, why:"A prodrome is a normal part of a canker sore. It does not require referral."},
  {t:"Fever, rash or swelling along with the sores", correct:true, why:"Symptoms of systemic illness are an exclusion, and they raise concern for infection."}],
 teach:"Referral triggers: lesions associated with underlying disease, lesions present more than 14 days, frequent recurrence, symptoms of systemic illness, failure of prior appropriate self-care, and no relief after 7 days of a debriding agent. A prodrome and eating pain are usual features.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 29, 40; T@59576, T@59992 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-026", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"nonpharm", concept:"lr-ras-ice",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which instruction about cold or heat for a canker sore is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"Apply a warm compress for 20 minutes at a time to ease the pain", correct:false, why:"Heat is not used. The handout says heat may spread the infection."},
  {t:"Apply ice after the lesion crusts over, the way a cold sore is managed", correct:false, why:"Crusting describes a cold sore. A canker sore is a gray ulcer, and ice can be used while it is painful."},
  {t:"Apply ice for 10 minutes at a time, up to 20 minutes each hour", correct:true, why:"She said ice, not heat. Ice is applied 10 minutes at a time, for no longer than 20 minutes in each hour."},
  {t:"Apply ice continuously for 60 minutes in each hour", correct:false, why:"The limit is 10-minute increments and no more than 20 minutes in each hour."}],
 teach:"Non-drug care for canker sores: avoid spicy, acidic and sharp-textured foods, apply ice directly to the lesion for 10 minutes at a time and no more than 20 minutes each hour, avoid heat, and correct any nutritional deficiency to deter recurrence.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 32; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@61228, T@61497 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-027", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"anes", concept:"lr-ras-anesthetic-combine",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Ng, 33, applies a benzocaine gel to his canker sore and wants to add a menthol lozenge. What do you tell him?",
 options:[
  {t:"Use the menthol first and the benzocaine 30 minutes later", correct:false, why:"Spacing does not remove the problem: the combination is to be avoided."},
  {t:"Add camphor as well, because up to 3% is allowed in the mix", correct:false, why:"The 3% camphor limit is for cold sore analgesics. For the canker sore anesthetic, the combination is to be avoided."},
  {t:"Combine them, because they relieve pain by different routes of action", correct:false, why:"Benzocaine or benzyl alcohol is not combined with menthol, phenol or camphor."},
  {t:"Avoid the combination, since it can irritate and damage tissue", correct:true, why:"She said not to use the anesthetic with menthol, phenol or camphor, since the two together can irritate and damage the tissue."}],
 teach:"Topical anesthetics (benzocaine, benzyl alcohol) give temporary pain relief, applied up to about four times a day. Do not combine them with menthol, phenol or camphor, since the pair irritates and can damage tissue.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 34; T@62160, T@62416 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-028", skill:"adverse", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"debr", concept:"lr-ras-debrid-7days",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why are oral debriding agents such as carbamide peroxide and hydrogen peroxide limited to 7 days?",
 options:[
  {t:"Over-cleansing lets opportunistic organisms overgrow", correct:true, why:"She said that over-cleansing allows opportunistic infection in, and gave black hairy tongue as a classic complaint."},
  {t:"Prolonged use raises the blood pressure through systemic absorption", correct:false, why:"This is a decongestant concern, not a debriding agent effect."},
  {t:"Prolonged use causes rebound redness after the agent is stopped", correct:false, why:"Rebound redness is the reason ophthalmic decongestants are limited to 3 days."},
  {t:"Prolonged use leaves a chemical burn from holding it on the lesion", correct:false, why:"This is the warning for aspirin held in the mouth."}],
 teach:"Debriding agents cleanse the mouth, so too much cleansing lets opportunistic organisms in. Prolonged use can cause tooth sensitivity, soft tissue irritation and black hairy tongue. The limit is 7 days, and a lack of relief by then means referral.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 36; T@64467, T@64645 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-029", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"debr", concept:"lr-ras-debrid-counsel", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Vega, 29, chooses carbamide peroxide for her canker sore. SELECT ALL key counseling points you give her.",
 options:[
  {t:"Continue for 14 days to prevent recurrence", correct:false, why:"The agent is limited to 7 days. It treats the existing lesion and does not prevent recurrence."},
  {t:"Avoid eating or drinking for at least 30 minutes afterward", correct:true, why:"The instructions on the slide say to avoid eating or drinking for at least 30 minutes after application."},
  {t:"Swallow the solution after holding it in the mouth", correct:false, why:"Direct application is held on the area and then spit out. It is not swallowed."},
  {t:"Stop after 7 days, and see a provider if the sore is not better", correct:true, why:"The limit is 7 days, because longer use risks opportunistic infection, and lack of relief by then means referral."},
  {t:"Use it after meals, three to four times a day", correct:true, why:"She said these products are used after a meal, 3 to 4 times a day depending on meal frequency."}],
 teach:"She said her exam will give the product and ask for key counseling points. For debriding agents: after meals, 3 to 4 times a day, nothing to eat or drink for 30 minutes, and no more than 7 days. Prolonged use can cause tooth sensitivity, soft tissue irritation and black hairy tongue.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 35, 36, 40; T@64227, T@64467, T@68095 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-030", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"debr", concept:"lr-ras-h2o2-hypersens",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Doyle, 38, has a canker sore and tooth hypersensitivity. He wants to use hydrogen peroxide. Which advice fits both problems?",
 options:[
  {t:"Use the 3% rinse mixed with equal water and swish for 1 minute", correct:false, why:"This is the standard rinse, which exposes the teeth. She advised against it with hypersensitivity."},
  {t:"Hold carbamide peroxide on the sensitive tooth for 1 minute", correct:false, why:"The agents are applied to the sore, not to the sensitive tooth."},
  {t:"Use the applicator on the sore and avoid the rinse", correct:true, why:"She said that with tooth hypersensitivity, use the applicator and not the rinse to avoid exposure of the teeth."},
  {t:"Swish with the rinse several times a day for 10 days", correct:false, why:"Both the rinse and the duration are wrong, since use is limited to 7 days and the rinse is avoided here."}],
 teach:"Tooth hypersensitivity links to the canker sore products. Peroxide rinses can sensitize teeth, so for a patient with hypersensitivity she said to apply with the applicator and avoid the rinse. A protectant or other option can also be chosen.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 36; T@65194, T@67059 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-031", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"prot", concept:"lr-ras-protectant",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which instruction for a topical oral protectant patch or disc is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"Use it before meals twice a day and rinse the mouth afterward to remove it", correct:false, why:"The protectant is used after meals and should not be rinsed off, since it needs time to adhere."},
  {t:"Use it up to 5 times a day until the lesion heals, with no drinking for 30 minutes", correct:false, why:"Five times a day until healed is the docosanol schedule for cold sores."},
  {t:"Use it after meals and spit it out after 1 minute, with no drinking for 30 minutes", correct:false, why:"Spitting out after about a minute is for debriding agents. The protectant stays in place and dissolves."},
  {t:"Use it up to 3 to 4 times a day after meals, with no drinking for 30 minutes", correct:true, why:"That is 3 to 4 times a day after meals and no drinking for up to 30 minutes after, so the barrier can form. It dissolves in 8 to 12 hours."}],
 teach:"A protectant coats the sore for temporary relief. It is a paste, patch or disc, used 3 to 4 times a day after meals, dissolving in about 8 to 12 hours, with nothing to drink for 30 minutes. Her slide and handout differ on how long to hold the disc, so that time is not asked.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 37; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@65892, T@65877 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-032", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"rinse", concept:"lr-ras-homemade",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient asks for a homemade remedy for a canker sore. Which is described correctly?",
 options:[
  {t:"A saline rinse of 1 to 3 teaspoons of salt in 4 to 8 ounces of water", correct:true, why:"This is the saline rinse on slide 38. It is one of several options that all work, chosen to fit the patient."},
  {t:"An aspirin tablet held against the sore until it dissolves in the mouth", correct:false, why:"Aspirin should not be held in the mouth, because it can cause a chemical burn."},
  {t:"An undiluted 3% hydrogen peroxide rinse swished for 1 minute and spit out", correct:false, why:"The 3% solution is mixed with an equal amount of water before rinsing."},
  {t:"A baking soda paste left on the lesion for 30 minutes or longer", correct:false, why:"The paste is applied for a few minutes, up to 4 times a day."}],
 teach:"Homemade options: a saline rinse (1 to 3 teaspoons salt in 4 to 8 ounces warm water) or a baking soda paste applied for a few minutes up to 4 times a day. She said the guidelines give no order of choice, since the products work about equally.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 38, 39; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@66527, T@66752 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-033", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"sys", concept:"lr-ras-aspirin",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Cole, 41, plans to dissolve an aspirin tablet on her canker sore for pain. What do you advise?",
 options:[
  {t:"Use it for a lesion that has crusted over, as in a cold sore", correct:false, why:"Crusting describes a cold sore, and aspirin should still not be held in the mouth."},
  {t:"Swallow it, and do not hold it on the lesion", correct:true, why:"She said not to hold aspirin to the lesion because it is irritating and can cause a chemical burn."},
  {t:"Crush it into a paste with baking soda and apply it to the sore", correct:false, why:"Baking soda paste is its own option, and aspirin in the mouth still burns the tissue."},
  {t:"Hold it on the sore, which gives the quickest relief", correct:false, why:"This is the error. Aspirin held in the mouth causes a chemical burn with tissue damage."}],
 teach:"NSAIDs or acetaminophen can add pain relief when swallowed. The warning is that aspirin tablets should not be retained in the mouth before swallowing, since they can cause a chemical burn and tissue damage.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 39; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@69768 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-034", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"refer", concept:"lr-ras-refer-7",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mrs. Bell, 52, has used a carbamide peroxide product for 7 days. The lesion is still painful, and it is day 10 of the sore. What is the next step?",
 options:[
  {t:"Reassure her and wait until day 14 with no further treatment needed", correct:false, why:"Her symptoms did not abate after 7 days of treatment, which already calls for referral."},
  {t:"Switch to docosanol 10% five times a day for 10 days", correct:false, why:"Docosanol is a cold sore antiviral. It does not treat a canker sore."},
  {t:"Refer her to her doctor, since 7 days of treatment did not help", correct:true, why:"Referral applies when symptoms do not abate after 7 days of a debriding agent, or when the lesion does not heal in 14 days."},
  {t:"Continue the same product for 7 more days", correct:false, why:"The product is limited to 7 days. Going on risks opportunistic infection."}],
 teach:"Referral for canker sores: symptoms do not abate after 7 days of a debriding agent, the lesion does not heal in 14 days, symptoms worsen during self-treatment, or signs of systemic infection such as fever, rash or swelling appear.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 40; T@69914 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-035", skill:"patho", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"path", concept:"lr-hsl-virus",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement about the cause of cold sores is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"They result from bacterial invasion that causes inflammation", correct:false, why:"This is the mechanism of toothache."},
  {t:"They are caused by HSV-2, which also causes canker sores", correct:false, why:"HSV-2 is associated with genital sores and is covered in the ID module. Canker sores have no known infectious cause."},
  {t:"They have no known cause, and certain triggers precipitate their appearance", correct:false, why:"This is the description of canker sores."},
  {t:"They are caused by HSV-1, which goes latent and then reactivates", correct:true, why:"Cold sores are caused by herpes simplex virus 1. After the first infection the virus goes latent, and triggers reactivate it."}],
 teach:"Cold sores (fever blisters, herpes simplex labialis) are caused by HSV-1, which is contagious by direct contact. After the first infection the virus goes latent and can reactivate. HSV-2 and genital herpes are covered by Dr. Beck in ID.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 43; T@70970 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-036", skill:"patho", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"path", concept:"lr-hsl-triggers", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Webb, 24, gets cold sores after finals. SELECT ALL factors on his list that can trigger a cold sore outbreak.",
 options:[
  {t:"Brushing with a firm-bristle brush", correct:false, why:"Hard brushing contributes to exposed dentin and sensitivity, not cold sores."},
  {t:"Biting the inside of his cheek", correct:false, why:"Local trauma of the mouth lining is a canker sore trigger, not a cold sore trigger."},
  {t:"Stress and fatigue from studying", correct:true, why:"Stress and fatigue are listed triggers that reactivate the latent virus."},
  {t:"A fever from a recent cold", correct:true, why:"Fever and other infectious illness are listed triggers."},
  {t:"Ultraviolet exposure from a day on the ski slopes", correct:true, why:"Ultraviolet radiation and windburn are listed triggers for herpes simplex labialis."}],
 teach:"Cold sore triggers are stimuli that reactivate latent HSV-1: ultraviolet radiation, stress, fatigue, cold, windburn, fever, injury, menstruation, dental work, infectious diseases and anything suppressing the immune system. Her case used cold, windy weather as the trigger.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 44, 45; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@71856 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-037", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"path", concept:"lr-hsl-secondary",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Ito, 36, has had a cold sore for 6 days. It has not crusted over and the border is still red. What is the best recommendation?",
 options:[
  {t:"Hydrocortisone 1% ointment as needed for the persistent redness", correct:false, why:"A cold sore is not steroid responsive, and topical steroids are on the do-not-recommend list."},
  {t:"Triple antibiotic ointment 3 to 4 times a day, plus PCP follow-up", correct:true, why:"Failure to crust and a persistent red border suggest secondary infection. Triple antibiotic ointment is used 3 to 4 times a day, and the PCP should follow up."},
  {t:"Camphor 5% gel three to four times a day after each meal for pain", correct:false, why:"Camphor above 3% is contraindicated, and it would not address infection."},
  {t:"Stop treatment, because a mature lesion has crusted and healed", correct:false, why:"The lesion has not crusted, which is the sign she described for a possible secondary infection."}],
 teach:"A normal mature cold sore crusts. A lesion that is not developing into a mature lesion, with no crust and a persistent red border, may have a secondary bacterial infection. She said then to recommend triple antibiotic ointment three to four times a day and to follow up with the PCP.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 45, 56; T@80722 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-038", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"assess", concept:"lr-hsl-noprev",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"A 19-year-old has a small blister on her upper lip that began yesterday. She has not had a cold sore before. Is she a candidate for self-care?",
 options:[
  {t:"Yes, and docosanol should be started at the first sign", correct:false, why:"A prior diagnosis is needed before self-care. Without one she is referred."},
  {t:"No, because the lesion has been present for over 14 days", correct:false, why:"The lesion began yesterday, so the 14-day limit is not what applies."},
  {t:"No, since she has no previous diagnosis of a cold sore", correct:true, why:"A first lesion without a prior diagnosis is an exclusion. She said pharmacists do not have the scope of practice to diagnose it yet."},
  {t:"Yes, and a topical steroid should be used to reduce the blister", correct:false, why:"A cold sore is not steroid responsive, and topical steroids are not recommended."}],
 teach:"Exclusions to self-care for cold sores: no previous diagnosis, lesions present more than 14 days, increased frequency of outbreaks, immunocompromised, and symptoms of systemic infection. A first-time lesion goes to a doctor before any product is chosen.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 49; T@75256 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-039", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"case", concept:"lr-hsl-case1-dx-candidate",
 tags:["poll", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"A 42-year-old patient has an uncomfortable sore on her lip. She gets these when stressed, usually once a month, but this one has lasted a month. It is crusted with fluid-filled pustules. What is the likely condition, and is she a candidate for self-care?",
 options:[
  {t:"Recurrent aphthous stomatitis; a candidate, because it heals by itself", correct:false, why:"Crusted fluid-filled lesions on the lip are a cold sore, and the lesion has outlasted normal healing."},
  {t:"Toothache; not a candidate, because the pain remains constant", correct:false, why:"Nothing in the stem describes tooth pain. The lesion is on the lip."},
  {t:"Herpes simplex labialis; a candidate, because she gets these with stress", correct:false, why:"The lesion has lasted well beyond the 14-day limit, so she is not a candidate despite the known pattern."},
  {t:"Herpes simplex labialis; not a candidate, since it has lasted a month", correct:true, why:"Location and triggers point to a cold sore. She said she is not a self-care candidate, since it has been there more than a month."}],
 teach:"This is her first case: red, fluid-filled, crusted lesions on the lip with stress as the trigger make it a cold sore. The duration then decides the path. A lesion present for a month is beyond the 14 days, so the answer is no, refer.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 62, 64, 49; T@83268, T@82982 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-040", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"case", concept:"lr-hsl-case2-select", multi:true,
 tags:["poll", "multi", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"A 21-year-old has a painful, small, red lesion outside her lower lip. Outbreaks follow cold, windy weather. Her last cold sore was a year ago, and the campus wellness center told her to use something over the counter. PMH: HSL. Allergies: NKDA. Medication: birth control. SELECT ALL of the appropriate recommendations for this patient.",
 options:[
  {t:"Cover up your face with a scarf when outside", correct:true, why:"Cold and wind are her trigger, so protecting the lips is the non-drug measure."},
  {t:"Docosanol 10% applied 5 times a day until the lesion heals", correct:true, why:"Docosanol is the first-line antiviral: 5 times a day until healed, up to 10 days."},
  {t:"Hydrocortisone 1% ointment as needed", correct:false, why:"She said a cold sore does not respond to steroids, and topical steroids are on the do-not-recommend list."},
  {t:"Camphor 5% gel 3 to 4 times a day after meals", correct:false, why:"She said the concentration is too high: camphor above 3% is contraindicated."},
  {t:"Benzocaine 5% gel, a thin layer 3 to 4 times a day after a meal", correct:true, why:"She said a topical analgesic can be added for pain. It is a plus or minus and does not shorten the infection."}],
 teach:"Her answer for this case: yes, a candidate, because she had a diagnosis and was told over the counter care was fine. Use the scarf, docosanol 10% at the first sign, and optionally a topical analgesic. A steroid does not work, and camphor above 3% is not allowed.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 54, 58, 65, 66, 67; T@83449, T@84763, T@84847, T@84973 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-041", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"case", concept:"lr-hsl-candidate-prior",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Ward, 27, was diagnosed with cold sores by her doctor. She felt tingling on her lip yesterday and has a small red lesion today. Her last outbreak was 10 months ago. She takes no daily medications. Is she a candidate for self-care, and why?",
 options:[
  {t:"No, because every cold sore must be referred to a doctor", correct:false, why:"Referral applies to exclusions such as no prior diagnosis, more than 14 days or systemic signs. She has none of them."},
  {t:"Yes, she has a diagnosis, a new lesion and no systemic signs", correct:true, why:"She has a diagnosis, outbreaks are not frequent, the lesion has not lasted 14 days, and she is not immunocompromised."},
  {t:"Yes, and she should start a topical steroid right away for the blister", correct:false, why:"A cold sore is not steroid responsive. The first-line product is docosanol."},
  {t:"No, because tingling means a secondary infection is present", correct:false, why:"Tingling is the prodrome, the best time to start docosanol. A secondary infection shows as no crusting and persistent redness."}],
 teach:"In her second case the candidate for self-care had a past diagnosis and occasional outbreaks, so she could be treated. What she counted as reoccurrence was once a month, not once a year. Check each exclusion in turn, and give the reason.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 49, 65; T@83449, T@75538 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-042", skill:"dose", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"anti", concept:"lr-hsl-docosanol-dose",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which directions for docosanol 10% cream are correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"Apply once a day at bedtime until the lesion crusts", correct:false, why:"The schedule is up to five times a day, started at the first sign."},
  {t:"Apply 3 to 4 times a day after meals for 7 days and avoid eating for 30 minutes", correct:false, why:"This resembles the analgesic and debriding agent directions, not docosanol."},
  {t:"Apply at the first sign, up to 5 times a day until healed, for 10 days", correct:true, why:"This is the slide and her words: first sign, up to 5 times a day until the lesion heals, and 10 days total."},
  {t:"Apply 5 times a day for 14 days regardless of healing", correct:false, why:"The course is until the lesion heals, for up to 10 days total, not a fixed 14 days."}],
 teach:"Docosanol 10% (Abreva) is first line. Start at the first sign, even before the lesion forms, use it up to 5 times a day until the lesion heals, and for 10 days total at most. Analgesics are used after meals 3 to 4 times a day instead.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 53; T@78875, T@79161 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-043", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"anti", concept:"lr-hsl-docosanol-timing",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Ford, 31, feels burning and tingling on her lip. There is no lesion yet. What do you tell her?",
 options:[
  {t:"Apply a hydrocortisone ointment to prevent the lesion from forming", correct:false, why:"A cold sore is not steroid responsive, and topical steroids are not recommended."},
  {t:"Apply a triple antibiotic ointment three to four times a day to prevent infection", correct:false, why:"Triple antibiotic ointment is for a suspected secondary infection, not a prodrome."},
  {t:"Wait for a blister to form before starting docosanol cream", correct:false, why:"Waiting loses the benefit. The antiviral is started at the first sign of infection."},
  {t:"Start docosanol now, at the first sign, before any lesion appears", correct:true, why:"She said to apply it at the first symptom, even before the lesion forms, when the burning prodrome starts."}],
 teach:"Docosanol prevents viral replication, so timing matters: use it at the first sign of infection, including the burning or tingling prodrome. It is the one product that reduces the duration and the severity of symptoms, which is why it is first line.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 53; T@78410, T@78875 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-044", skill:"mech", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"anti", concept:"lr-hsl-docosanol-moa",
 tags:["hard"], source:"slide",
 stem:"How does docosanol 10% work against a cold sore?",
 options:[
  {t:"It blocks viral fusion with the cell membrane", correct:true, why:"The handout gives this mechanism, and slide 53 states the purpose as preventing viral replication."},
  {t:"It depolarizes the excited nerves so pain signals stop", correct:false, why:"This is the mechanism of potassium nitrate in desensitizing toothpaste."},
  {t:"It forms a barrier that coats and protects the sore from irritants", correct:false, why:"This is the purpose of a topical oral protectant for canker sores."},
  {t:"It replaces protective components that natural saliva lacks", correct:false, why:"This is the purpose of artificial saliva for dry mouth."}],
 teach:"Docosanol is a topical antiviral. It blocks fusion of the virus with the host cell membrane, so the virus cannot replicate. That is why it shortens the infection and is used at the first sign, before many cells are infected.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 53; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf)"},

{id:"LOR-045", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"analg", concept:"lr-hsl-analgesic-role",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which product for a cold sore reduces the duration of the infection, rather than just relieving pain?",
 options:[
  {t:"Benzocaine gel", correct:false, why:"A topical anesthetic relieves burning, itching and pain. It does not reduce the duration."},
  {t:"Docosanol 10%", correct:true, why:"It is the topical antiviral and reduces both the duration and the severity of symptoms."},
  {t:"Camphor gel", correct:false, why:"A topical analgesic relieves discomfort. She said analgesics relieve pain and do not do both."},
  {t:"Acetaminophen", correct:false, why:"A systemic analgesic gives additional pain relief, nothing more."}],
 teach:"Match product to goal. Reduce the duration of the infection: topical antiviral (docosanol). Relieve pain: topical analgesic or anesthetic, or a systemic analgesic. The analgesic is an add-on, with a plus or minus use, not first line.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 52, 53, 54; T@78159, T@78410 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-046", skill:"dose", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"analg", concept:"lr-hsl-camphor-limit",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which topical analgesic for cold sore pain is within the allowed concentration?",
 options:[
  {t:"Camphor 5% rub, such as Vicks VapoRub", correct:false, why:"She said Vicks has about 5% camphor, so do not use it for a cold sore."},
  {t:"Menthol 2% gel applied after meals", correct:false, why:"Menthol above 1% is contraindicated."},
  {t:"Camphor 3% gel applied after meals", correct:true, why:"The slide contraindicates camphor above 3% and menthol above 1%, so camphor at 3% is allowed."},
  {t:"Menthol 1.5% lozenge used after meals", correct:false, why:"Menthol above 1% is not allowed, so 1.5% exceeds the limit."}],
 teach:"Topical analgesics for cold sores (benzocaine, dibucaine, dyclonine, benzyl alcohol, camphor, menthol) are applied after meals with nothing to eat or drink for 30 minutes. Camphor above 3% and menthol above 1% are contraindicated, as they produce a counterirritant effect.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 54; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@79683, T@80226 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-047", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"avoid", concept:"lr-hsl-steroid",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient asks to put hydrocortisone 1% ointment on his cold sore. What is the best response?",
 options:[
  {t:"Recommend it with a camphor 5% gel to relieve the itching", correct:false, why:"Camphor above 3% is contraindicated, and the steroid is not recommended."},
  {t:"Recommend it once the lesion has crusted over", correct:false, why:"Steroids are not recommended at any stage of a cold sore."},
  {t:"Recommend it for 7 days as the first-line product for the lesion", correct:false, why:"It is not first line. Docosanol is the first-line antiviral."},
  {t:"Do not recommend it, since a cold sore is not steroid responsive", correct:true, why:"Topical steroids are on the do-not-recommend list. She said the herpes virus does not respond to corticosteroids."}],
 teach:"A cold sore is a viral lesion, so a corticosteroid does not treat it, and topical steroids are highly astringent. They are on her do-not-recommend list. The first-line product is docosanol, with an optional topical analgesic.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 58; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@81817 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-048", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"avoid", concept:"lr-hsl-complementary",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient asks whether lysine, zinc or tea tree oil can prevent or treat cold sores. What do the guidelines say?",
 options:[
  {t:"They are not recommended, since there is not enough data", correct:true, why:"She said there is not enough data, and the guidelines do not recommend these products to prevent or treat cold sores."},
  {t:"They are recommended in place of docosanol for children", correct:false, why:"The guidelines do not recommend them for anyone, and slide 57 gives them no role."},
  {t:"They are recommended as first-line prevention with docosanol", correct:false, why:"Docosanol is first line, and the complementary therapies are not recommended."},
  {t:"They are recommended if outbreaks become more frequent", correct:false, why:"Frequent outbreaks are an exclusion to self-care, not a reason to use these products."}],
 teach:"Tea tree oil, lysine and zinc appear on the complementary therapy slide. Data are conflicting or limited, so the guidelines do not recommend them to prevent or treat cold sores. The evidence-backed options are docosanol and the analgesics.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 57; T@81198, T@81464 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-049", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"nonpharm", concept:"lr-hsl-nonpharm-select", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi"], source:"both",
 stem:"SELECT ALL non-drug measures for a cold sore.",
 options:[
  {t:"Wash the hands frequently", correct:true, why:"Frequent handwashing prevents spread of the lesion contamination."},
  {t:"Apply ice in 10-minute increments up to 20 minutes each hour", correct:false, why:"This is the canker sore measure, along with avoiding heat."},
  {t:"Keep the lesion clean with gentle washing and mild soap", correct:true, why:"Keeping lesions clean prevents contamination and secondary infection."},
  {t:"Wear an SPF lip protectant outdoors", correct:true, why:"Sun exposure is a trigger, so sunscreen or a covering protects the lips."},
  {t:"Avoid spicy, acidic and sharp-textured foods", correct:false, why:"This is non-drug advice for a canker sore ulcer inside the mouth."}],
 teach:"Non-drug care for cold sores: avoid triggers (sunscreen or SPF lip protectant, a scarf), keep the involved skin moist so it does not crack, keep lesions clean with mild soap, and wash hands often to prevent spread.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 50; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@76354, T@76913 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-050", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-hsl", sub:"follow", concept:"lr-hsl-refer-select", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"SELECT ALL findings in a patient with a cold sore that call for referral to the PCP.",
 options:[
  {t:"Pain eased within 2 days of starting benzocaine", correct:false, why:"Relief of discomfort is the goal, so this is a response, not a reason to refer."},
  {t:"The lesion crusted over by day 9", correct:false, why:"Crusting is the expected course of a mature lesion."},
  {t:"The lesion has not healed after 14 days", correct:true, why:"Healing normally occurs within 10 to 14 days, so lesions that do not heal in 14 days need a provider."},
  {t:"Fever, malaise, rash or swollen lymph glands appear", correct:true, why:"These are signs or symptoms of systemic illness and call for the PCP."},
  {t:"Lesions recur despite appropriate self-care", correct:true, why:"Recurrence despite appropriate self-care is on the referral list."}],
 teach:"Cold sores are self-limiting and heal within 10 to 14 days. Refer if the lesion does not heal in 14 days, recurs despite appropriate care, the measures do not relieve discomfort, signs of systemic illness appear, or symptoms change or worsen.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 59, 60; T@81969 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-051", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-diff", sub:"diff", concept:"lr-diff-ras-case",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Cho, 20, has a round, gray ulcer with a red border on the inside of her lower lip. It stings when she eats. Which condition is most likely?",
 options:[
  {t:"Herpes simplex labialis (cold sore)", correct:false, why:"A cold sore is outside the mouth, around the lips, as red fluid-filled vesicles that crust."},
  {t:"Toothache", correct:false, why:"This is constant pain from bacterial invasion, not a mucosal ulcer."},
  {t:"Tooth hypersensitivity", correct:false, why:"This is a sharp pain from exposed dentin on exposure to a stimulus, not an ulcer."},
  {t:"Recurrent aphthous stomatitis", correct:true, why:"Inside the mouth on the movable mucosa, gray, round or oval, with a red halo: this is a canker sore."}],
 teach:"Her differentiation is location first, then looks. Canker sore: inside the mouth, usually gray or yellow, round or oval, flat or crater-like, with an erythematous halo. Cold sore: outside the mouth or around the lips, with red fluid-filled vesicles.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 27, 47; T@73857 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-052", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-diff", sub:"diff", concept:"lr-diff-hsl-case",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Dunn, 45, has a cluster of small red blisters on the edge of his upper lip that burst and crust. Which condition is most likely?",
 options:[
  {t:"Herpes simplex labialis (cold sore)", correct:true, why:"Red, fluid-filled vesicles at the junction of the lip and skin that burst and crust are a cold sore."},
  {t:"Toothache", correct:false, why:"This is bacterial tooth pain that persists without a stimulus, not a lip lesion."},
  {t:"Recurrent aphthous stomatitis (canker sore)", correct:false, why:"A canker sore is an ulcer inside the mouth, gray or yellow, not blisters on the lip border."},
  {t:"Tooth hypersensitivity", correct:false, why:"This condition causes pain on exposure to stimuli, with no visible lesion."}],
 teach:"Locate first. Outside the mouth, around the lips, with red fluid-filled vesicles that coalesce and crust is a cold sore. She said this is what you need to know to differentiate the two, and the handout table lists the lip and nose junction as the site.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 45, 47; T@73857 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-053", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-diff", sub:"diff", concept:"lr-diff-pair",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which pairing of condition and presentation is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"Cold sore: a flat, crater-like ulcer on the tongue and soft palate", correct:false, why:"This is the major canker sore description, which occurs inside the mouth."},
  {t:"Canker sore: a gray, round ulcer inside the mouth on movable mucosa", correct:true, why:"The canker sore is an ulcer inside the mouth on non-keratinized mucosa such as the tongue, floor of the mouth and inside of the lips and cheeks."},
  {t:"Cold sore: a gray, round ulcer inside the cheek with a red halo", correct:false, why:"This is the canker sore presentation."},
  {t:"Canker sore: red fluid-filled vesicles at the junction of lip and skin", correct:false, why:"This is the cold sore presentation."}],
 teach:"Pair location with look. Canker sore: inside the mouth, non-keratinized mucosa, round or oval gray or yellow ulcer with a red halo. Cold sore: outside the mouth at the lip and skin junction, red fluid-filled vesicles, crusted when mature.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 27, 47; T@58338, T@73857 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-054", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-diff", sub:"diff", concept:"lr-diff-hsl-select", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi"], source:"both",
 stem:"SELECT ALL features that point to a cold sore rather than a canker sore.",
 options:[
  {t:"It forms on non-keratinized mucosa of the tongue and cheeks", correct:false, why:"This is the usual canker sore location."},
  {t:"It starts as red fluid-filled vesicles", correct:true, why:"Cold sore vesicles are red and fluid-filled, and they coalesce and crust when mature."},
  {t:"It appears outside the mouth, on or around the lips", correct:true, why:"She said cold sores are outside of the mouth or around the lips, canker sores inside."},
  {t:"It is contagious by direct contact", correct:true, why:"Cold sores are HSV-1 infections transmitted by direct contact."},
  {t:"It is a gray, oval ulcer with a red halo", correct:false, why:"This is the canker sore description."}],
 teach:"Cold sore: outside, around the lips; red fluid-filled vesicles that crust; HSV-1, contagious. Canker sore: inside the mouth; gray or yellow oval ulcer with a halo; no known infectious cause, not contagious as a cold sore is.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 43, 47; T@70970, T@73857 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-055", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-diff", sub:"treat", concept:"lr-diff-abreva-wrong",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Tate, 26, has a round gray ulcer inside his cheek and asks for Abreva. What is the best response?",
 options:[
  {t:"Recommend docosanol at the first sign, 5 times a day for up to 10 days", correct:false, why:"This is the cold sore regimen, and the lesion is inside the mouth."},
  {t:"Recommend a hydrocortisone 1% ointment on the ulcer twice a day", correct:false, why:"Hydrocortisone is not recommended for cold sores, and it is not a canker sore option."},
  {t:"Recommend triple antibiotic ointment three times a day on the ulcer", correct:false, why:"This is for a cold sore with suspected secondary infection, not for an ulcer inside the mouth."},
  {t:"Explain that docosanol is for cold sores; offer a canker sore product", correct:true, why:"The ulcer is a canker sore. Docosanol is a cold sore antiviral, and canker sores are treated for pain relief with the options on slides 33 to 38."}],
 teach:"Identify the condition before choosing a product. A canker sore needs pain relief and protection (anesthetic, debriding agent, protectant, rinse), while a cold sore needs docosanol at the first sign. Matching the product to the wrong condition is the usual trap.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 33, 47, 51; T@73857, T@77492 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-056", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-diff", sub:"treat", concept:"lr-diff-hsl-first-line",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Lowe, 28, has small red vesicles at the junction of her lip and skin. A friend suggested a carbamide peroxide rinse as for a canker sore. What do you recommend?",
 options:[
  {t:"Docosanol 10% cream at the first sign, plus an optional analgesic", correct:true, why:"The lesion is a cold sore, so the first-line product is docosanol, with an analgesic that is a plus or minus."},
  {t:"Carbamide peroxide 10-15% as an oral rinse after each meal for 7 days", correct:false, why:"This is a canker sore debriding agent. It does not treat a viral lesion on the lip border."},
  {t:"A saline rinse of 1 to 3 teaspoons of salt in warm water up to 4 times a day", correct:false, why:"This is a canker sore rinse for inside the mouth."},
  {t:"A protectant disc applied after meals to coat the sore", correct:false, why:"Protectants coat canker sore ulcers inside the mouth. It does not reduce the duration of a cold sore."}],
 teach:"Cold sore: first line is topical docosanol, with a topical analgesic as an option and triple antibiotic for suspected secondary infection. Canker sore: any of the pain-relief products. The location and appearance tell you which set to use.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 47, 51, 53; T@73857, T@77492 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-058", skill:"class", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"risk", concept:"lr-dry-select-rebuilt", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mrs. Perez, 74, has dry mouth. Her list includes amitriptyline, hydroxyzine, risperidone, docosanol 10% cream and a potassium nitrate toothpaste. SELECT ALL of her products that can induce dry mouth.",
 options:[
  {t:"Hydroxyzine", correct:true, why:"An antihistamine with anticholinergic action. Antihistamines are on her slide of medications that cause dry mouth."},
  {t:"Potassium nitrate toothpaste", correct:false, why:"A desensitizing toothpaste active. It is not on her list of medications that cause dry mouth."},
  {t:"Amitriptyline", correct:true, why:"A tricyclic antidepressant with anticholinergic action, on the handout list of drugs that reduce salivation."},
  {t:"Docosanol 10% cream", correct:false, why:"A topical antiviral for cold sores. It is not on her list of medications that cause dry mouth."},
  {t:"Risperidone", correct:true, why:"An antipsychotic, a class on the slide of medications that can cause dry mouth."}],
 teach:"Think in classes, as on slide 72: antihistamines, decongestants, antihypertensives, diuretics, antidepressants, antipsychotics and sedatives. The handout adds named drugs, such as amitriptyline, hydroxyzine, risperidone, oxybutynin and clonidine. Topical products from the other conditions are not on the list.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 72; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@85829, T@86368 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-059", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"risk", concept:"lr-dry-which-med",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Pruitt, 71, has had a dry mouth for a month. He takes diphenhydramine for sleep, uses a potassium nitrate toothpaste, applies docosanol cream for cold sores and uses benzocaine gel for a canker sore. Which product is the most likely cause?",
 options:[
  {t:"Docosanol 10% cream", correct:false, why:"It is a topical antiviral for cold sores, applied at the first sign, and is not on the list of dry mouth causes."},
  {t:"Benzocaine gel", correct:false, why:"It is a topical anesthetic for sores, and is not on the list of dry mouth causes."},
  {t:"Potassium nitrate toothpaste", correct:false, why:"It is a desensitizing active for dentin sensitivity, not a cause of dry mouth."},
  {t:"Diphenhydramine at bedtime", correct:true, why:"It is an antihistamine with anticholinergic properties. She said Benadryl is usually the answer when a drug causes dry mouth."}],
 teach:"The patient asks which medication is causing the dry mouth. Look for antihistamines, decongestants, antihypertensives, diuretics, antidepressants, antipsychotics and sedatives. Diphenhydramine is the example she singled out, through its anticholinergic effect.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 72; T@85829, T@86368 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-060", skill:"mech", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"risk", concept:"lr-dry-mechanism",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"By which two mechanisms do medications cause dry mouth?",
 options:[
  {t:"Anticholinergic activity, or depleted salivary flow volume", correct:true, why:"Drug-induced dry mouth comes from anticholinergic activity or from a drug that depletes the volume of salivary flow."},
  {t:"Prolonged over-cleansing, or tissue irritation of the lining", correct:false, why:"These are adverse effects of oral debriding agents on the mouth."},
  {t:"Viral latency, or reactivation by a known trigger", correct:false, why:"These describe how cold sores recur."},
  {t:"Opening of dentin tubules, or bacterial invasion of the pulp", correct:false, why:"These describe tooth hypersensitivity and toothache."}],
 teach:"She broke drug-related dry mouth into two mechanisms: an anticholinergic property, as with diphenhydramine and tiotropium, or the drug depleting the volume of salivary flow. That is why she wants you to be able to name which medications could be responsible.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 72; T@86018, T@86741 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-061", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"pres", concept:"lr-dry-presentation", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi"], source:"both",
 stem:"SELECT ALL findings that fit the clinical presentation of dry mouth.",
 options:[
  {t:"Thrush", correct:true, why:"Thrush and an increased risk of dental caries are on the slide."},
  {t:"Red fluid-filled vesicles at the junction of the lip and skin", correct:false, why:"This is a cold sore."},
  {t:"Pain that remains even in the absence of a stimulus", correct:false, why:"This is the symptom of toothache."},
  {t:"Difficulty talking, chewing and swallowing", correct:true, why:"Difficulty talking, chewing and swallowing is the first finding on the slide."},
  {t:"Halitosis", correct:true, why:"Bad breath is on the slide, along with plaque accumulation."}],
 teach:"Dry mouth leads to difficulty talking, chewing and swallowing, dry lips, loss of appetite and taste, plaque, halitosis, stomatitis, thrush and a higher risk of dental caries. She said patients may develop many of these complications, including lots of cavities and bad breath.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 76; T@87279 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-062", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"assess", concept:"lr-dry-goals",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Abel, 66, has dry mouth from her medications. Which set of treatment goals applies?",
 options:[
  {t:"Reduce the infection duration, relieve pain and curtail its spread", correct:false, why:"These are cold sore goals."},
  {t:"Relieve pain and prevent complications while the lesion resolves", correct:false, why:"These are canker sore goals."},
  {t:"Remove the offending agent, relieve discomfort, lower decay risk", correct:true, why:"She said the goal is to remove the offending agent, relieve the discomfort and reduce the risk of dental cavities."},
  {t:"Repair the damaged tooth surface and minimize abrasion", correct:false, why:"These are tooth hypersensitivity goals."}],
 teach:"Each condition has its own goals. Dry mouth: remove the offending agent, relieve the discomfort, reduce dental decay risk. Tooth hypersensitivity: repair the tooth surface, minimize abrasion. Canker sore: pain relief, prevent complications. Cold sore: shorten infection, relieve pain, prevent secondary infection, limit spread.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 78, 12, 30, 48; T@87490 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-063", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"nonpharm", concept:"lr-dry-med-timing",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Quinn, 59, is stable on an anti-anxiety drug that dries his mouth. You do not feel comfortable telling him to stop it. What can he do?",
 options:[
  {t:"Take the drug one hour after meals so the food is digested", correct:false, why:"The timing is one hour before the meal, not after."},
  {t:"Take it with a sodium-containing artificial saliva spray at meals", correct:false, why:"Artificial saliva does not change when to take the drug, and sodium-containing products are for avoidance on low-sodium diets."},
  {t:"Take the drug with the meal so the food counteracts the dryness", correct:false, why:"The advice is to take it before the meal, so that the meal stimulates saliva afterward."},
  {t:"Take the drug one hour before meals so eating stimulates saliva", correct:true, why:"She said to take the medication one hour before meals, since the meal increases salivary gland activity."}],
 teach:"If a drug causes dry mouth and cannot be stopped, take it an hour before meals. Eating then stimulates the salivary glands. Other measures: avoid tobacco, alcohol and alcohol-containing mouth rinses, caffeine and hot, spicy food; chew sugar-free gum; drink water.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 81; T@88518 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-064", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"nonpharm", concept:"lr-dry-avoid-select", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Reid, 62, has dry mouth. SELECT ALL of her habits that you advise her to avoid.",
 options:[
  {t:"Using an alcohol-containing mouth rinse", correct:true, why:"She said alcohol, including alcohol in mouth rinses, contributes to dry mouth."},
  {t:"Drinking several cups of coffee a day", correct:true, why:"Caffeine is on the list of substances to avoid with dry mouth."},
  {t:"Drinking more water, especially fluoridated water", correct:false, why:"Water is recommended, especially if fluoridated."},
  {t:"Smoking", correct:true, why:"Tobacco, smoke and smokeless, is on the list of substances to avoid."},
  {t:"Chewing sugar-free gum", correct:false, why:"Gum stimulates the salivary glands and is recommended."}],
 teach:"Remove or reduce what dries the mouth: tobacco, alcohol including mouth rinses, caffeine and hot, spicy food. To add moisture: sugar-free gum, more water (especially fluoridated), a humidifier, and a very soft toothbrush to avoid tissue abrasion.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 81; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@87839, T@88997 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-065", skill:"class", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"pharm", concept:"lr-dry-saliva-role",
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement about artificial saliva products is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"They treat the underlying cause of the dry mouth", correct:false, why:"The cause is addressed by removing the offending drug or condition, not by artificial saliva."},
  {t:"They replace saliva but are not a cure", correct:true, why:"She said they are a band-aid: they relieve discomfort but do not have the protective components that the mouth produces."},
  {t:"They stimulate saliva production the way sugar-free gum does", correct:false, why:"Gum stimulates the glands. Artificial saliva is a replacement, so it is not a stimulant."},
  {t:"They restore normal salivary gland function over time with use", correct:false, why:"They replace saliva and do not restore gland function. She said they are not a cure."}],
 teach:"Artificial saliva (sprays, gels, gums, toothpaste) is replacement therapy. It relieves discomfort but is not a cure, since it lacks the protective components of natural saliva. Treating the cause, usually the offending medication, comes first.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 82; T@89366 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-066", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"pharm", concept:"lr-dry-sodium-select", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Hale, 69, has heart failure and follows a low-sodium diet. SELECT ALL artificial saliva ingredients he should avoid.",
 options:[
  {t:"Sodium carboxymethylcellulose", correct:true, why:"It is starred on slide 82 as a sodium-containing ingredient to avoid on a sodium-restricted diet."},
  {t:"Xylitol", correct:false, why:"It is an active ingredient without sodium, so it is not on the avoid list."},
  {t:"Sodium monofluorophosphate", correct:true, why:"It is the other starred ingredient, and is to be avoided on a sodium-restricted diet."},
  {t:"Isomalt", correct:false, why:"It is an active ingredient without sodium, so it is not starred."},
  {t:"Sorbitol", correct:false, why:"It is an active ingredient without sodium, so it is not starred."}],
 teach:"On slide 82 the ingredients with sodium are starred: sodium carboxymethylcellulose and sodium monofluorophosphate. For patients with severe blood pressure problems or heart failure, or any low-sodium diet, avoid products that contain sodium.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 82, 83; T@89910 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-067", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"pharm", concept:"lr-dry-saliva-use",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Ms. Lin, 70, asks how often to use an artificial saliva gel. Which directions are correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"Use it for no more than 7 days, since longer use over-cleanses", correct:false, why:"The 7-day limit applies to oral debriding agents. Artificial saliva has no such limit on her slide."},
  {t:"Use it 5 times a day until the dryness resolves, then stop", correct:false, why:"Five times a day until healed is the docosanol directions. Artificial saliva is used any time as needed."},
  {t:"Use a 1-inch strip on a soft brush twice daily for 4 to 6 weeks", correct:false, why:"This is the desensitizing toothpaste instruction."},
  {t:"Use it anytime, at least after meals and before bed", correct:true, why:"The instructions are to use it any time, at minimum after meals and before bed. The gel is a half-inch length spread through the mouth."}],
 teach:"Artificial saliva has simple directions: use it at any time, at least after meals and before bed. A gel is a half-inch length placed on the tongue and spread thoroughly through the mouth. It is a long-term comfort measure with evaluation at 5 to 7 days.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 82, 83; T@89837 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-068", skill:"case", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"follow", concept:"lr-dry-followup",
 tags:["hard", "case"], source:"both",
 stem:"Mr. Soto, 63, has used artificial saliva and non-drug measures for 6 days. His dryness is worse and he has a new mouth problem. What do you recommend?",
 options:[
  {t:"Refer him to his primary care provider to find the cause", correct:true, why:"If dryness worsens or additional symptoms or complications develop, he is referred to the PCP to be evaluated for the cause."},
  {t:"Continue artificial saliva for another 2 weeks without a change in plan", correct:false, why:"Continuing is for patients whose dryness has lessened. His has worsened."},
  {t:"Start docosanol 10% cream five times a day for 10 days", correct:false, why:"Docosanol treats cold sores and has no role in dry mouth."},
  {t:"Add a second artificial saliva product containing sodium", correct:false, why:"More saliva substitute does not address worsening dryness or new symptoms."}],
 teach:"Evaluate after 5 to 7 days of self-treatment. If the dryness has lessened, continue the artificial saliva, fluoride products and non-drug measures. If it gets worse or new symptoms or complications develop, send the patient to the PCP.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 84; T@90270, T@90534 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-069", skill:"tell", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-dry", sub:"risk", concept:"lr-dry-risk-select", multi:true,
 tags:["hard", "multi"], source:"both",
 stem:"SELECT ALL risk factors or possible etiologies for dry mouth.",
 options:[
  {t:"Latent HSV-1 infection", correct:false, why:"Latent HSV-1 relates to cold sores, not to dry mouth."},
  {t:"An older patient taking multiple medications", correct:true, why:"Older patients on multiple medications are listed as at risk."},
  {t:"Loss of enamel from acidic drinks", correct:false, why:"Enamel loss relates to tooth hypersensitivity."},
  {t:"Radiation therapy of the head and neck", correct:true, why:"Radiation therapy to the head or neck is on the slide."},
  {t:"Diabetes mellitus", correct:true, why:"Diabetes is on her slide of disease states that can cause dry mouth."}],
 teach:"Risk factors and etiologies on slide 72: disease states (diabetes mellitus, depression, with Sjogren syndrome and Crohn disease in the handout), radiation therapy of the head or neck, drugs from the listed classes, and older patients on multiple medications.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 72; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@85458 11/20"},

{id:"LOR-070", skill:"counsel", prof:'Lam', tier:'new', topic:"lr-ras", sub:"prot", concept:"lr-ras-disc-adhere", lowYield:true,
 tags:["hard"], source:"both",
 note:"Held out of scored practice: slide 37 prints 'Disc placement - 10-20 seconds' while the handout (10-20 minutes to adhere) and her recording (T@65877, 'about 10 to 20 minutes') say minutes. She did not correct the slide aloud, so the key is not unambiguous.",
 stem:"Per her handout and her recording, how long is a protectant disc placed against a canker sore so that it adheres?",
 options:[
  {t:"30 minutes", correct:false, why:"This is how long to avoid eating or drinking after placing the disc."},
  {t:"10 to 20 seconds", correct:false, why:"This is the time printed on slide 37, which differs from her handout and her spoken explanation."},
  {t:"10 to 20 minutes", correct:true, why:"Her handout and her spoken explanation give 10 to 20 minutes to adhere, after which a barrier forms and dissolves in 8 to 12 hours."},
  {t:"8 to 12 hours", correct:false, why:"This is how long the disc takes to dissolve, not how long it is held in place."}],
 teach:"Her three sources differ on the placement time: the slide prints 10 to 20 seconds, while the handout and her recording say 10 to 20 minutes. The dissolve time (8 to 12 hours) and the 30-minute wait for food and drink are the same in all three.",
 cite:"Lecture 13 (Lam_OralDisorders_slides.pdf) slides 37; handout (Lam_OralDisorders_handout.pdf); T@65877 11/20"});
